Q: Why does ios_base offer an iword array and a pword array? A: <p>The use case for <code>xalloc</code> and <code>iword</code> / <code>pword</code> is rather specific, but it solves a real problem: Communication between <code>iomanip</code> objects and <code>operator&lt;&lt;</code> / <code>operator&gt;&gt;</code> overloads.</p>
<p>Let's say your <code>MyClass</code> could be written in two different ways:</p>
<pre><code>MyClass x { /* ... */ };
std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; std::endl;
std::cout &lt;&lt; MyClass::alternate;
std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; std::endl;
</code></pre>
<p>So: Where does the iomanip (<code>MyClass::alternate</code>) store the information that alternate mode has been set for <code>std::cout</code>, so that the <code>operator&lt;&lt;( ostream &amp;, MyClass &amp; )</code> overload can pick it up and format accordingly?</p>
<p>You can't store it statically in <code>MyClass</code>, because it should only affect <code>std::cout</code>, not all output of any <code>MyClass</code>.</p>
<p>The answer is, you use <code>xalloc</code> to get a static, global ID for your &quot;MyClass formatting&quot; setting. Your iomanip sets it (using either <code>pword</code> or <code>iword</code>), and your <code>operator&lt;&lt;</code> overload reads it.</p>
